::ENG3_U03_L04_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: Argument A says the city should plant trees along the main road because shade will make walking safer during hot months. Argument B says the city should build covered bus shelters first because many residents wait outside in heat and rain every day. What shared topic do the arguments address? {
~A. Whether roads should be closed.#Neither argument says roads should close. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. How the city should protect people from weather while traveling.#Correct. Both arguments address public comfort and safety during travel. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. Whether trees are always harmful.#Argument A supports trees. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Whether buses should stop running.#Argument B supports bus shelters, not ending buses. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: Argument A says the city should plant trees along the main road because shade will make walking safer during hot months. Argument B says the city should build covered bus shelters first because many residents wait outside in heat and rain every day. What is Argument A's claim? {
~A. The city should build bus shelters first.#That is Argument B's claim. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Residents never walk.#Argument A focuses on walking. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. The city should plant trees along the main road.#Correct. That is the position Argument A supports. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. Rain is the only concern.#Argument A focuses on hot months and shade. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: Argument A says the city should plant trees along the main road because shade will make walking safer during hot months. Argument B says the city should build covered bus shelters first because many residents wait outside in heat and rain every day. What is Argument B's reasoning? {
~A. Trees improve shade for walkers.#That is Argument A's reasoning. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Bus shelters should be avoided.#Argument B supports bus shelters. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Walking is never safe.#Argument B focuses on people waiting outside. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. Covered shelters would protect residents who wait for buses in heat and rain.#Correct. This explains why Argument B supports shelters. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: Argument A says the city should plant trees along the main road because shade will make walking safer during hot months. Argument B says the city should build covered bus shelters first because many residents wait outside in heat and rain every day. Which argument is more focused on immediate daily need? {
=A. Argument B, because it addresses residents waiting outside every day in heat and rain.#Correct. The daily waiting detail makes the need immediate. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. Argument A, because trees grow instantly.#Trees do not provide instant shade. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Neither, because both arguments lack claims.#Both arguments have claims. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Argument A, because it ignores weather.#Argument A addresses hot months. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: Argument A says the city should plant trees along the main road because shade will make walking safer during hot months. Argument B says the city should build covered bus shelters first because many residents wait outside in heat and rain every day. Which evaluation is strongest? {
~A. Argument A is wrong because trees are boring.#This is personal preference, not evaluation. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Argument B is wrong because shelters have roofs.#That detail supports, not weakens, the reasoning. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
